// ============================================================================
// tract package demo -- a complete bilingual Z-fold gospel tract, imposed for
// printing, folding, and cutting.
//
// The tract package OWNS the page geometry: it sets paperwidth/paperheight, the
// page margins, and hsize/vsize itself so that picture coordinates are true
// sheet coordinates. For that reason this demo does NOT \use{document} -- that
// would fight the package over who controls the page. We \use{tract} and drive
// it entirely through the package's own \tract macro and configuration knobs.
//
// What \tract does: you write each language's message once as ordinary flowing
// prose. The package typesets each at the column content width, then \vsplits
// it into three panel-height columns (leftmost fills first). It places those
// columns across a full-sheet \picture and emits TWO pages -- the FRONT in
// language A and the BACK in language B, in the SAME column order. Printed
// double-sided (long-edge flip), the back is NOT mirrored, so B's columns land
// directly behind A's. Unfolded flat, one face reads language A across columns
// 1-2-3 and the other reads language B.
// ============================================================================

\use{tract}

// ---- configuration knobs (every public option shown) -----------------------

// Paper size. Two impositions ship with the package:
//   letter -- US Letter portrait holding TWO identical tracts stacked top and
//             bottom (each 612x396 pts), with a cut guide across the middle.
//             Cut the sheet in half to get two tracts. (the primary result here)
//   a5     -- A5 landscape (595.276x419.528 pts) holding ONE tract that fills
//             the sheet; no cut. To render that variant instead, change the
//             line below to:  \set tractpaper {a5}
\set tractpaper {letter}

// Inset from each panel edge to its content, in points (18pt = 0.25in).
\set tractmargin {18}

// Draw fold/cut tick marks in the margins so the folder and trimmer can line a
// straightedge up to them, leaving the printed faces clean. Set to {0} for a
// completely clean sheet with no guides at all.
\set tractguides {1}

// Colour of the guide ticks (any CSS colour name or #RRGGBB hex), and the
// length of each tick in points.
\set tractguidecolor {gray}
\set tracttick {14}

// Hyphenation language per face. Each value is a tag whose patterns ship in the
// binary (en-us, es, fr). A narrow tract column justifies far more tightly when
// long words can break, so we set each face to its body language: English on
// the front, Spanish on the back. An empty value (the default) leaves a face
// unhyphenated. For a language not bundled, \loadhyphenation{tag}{path} once
// before \tract, then name the tag here.
\set tractlanga {en-us}
\set tractlangb {es}

// ---- a note on print registration ------------------------------------------
// Print this PDF DOUBLE-SIDED, flip on the LONG edge, at TRUE 100% (no
// fit-to-page scaling), e.g.:
//   lp -d <printer> -o sides=two-sided-long-edge -o media=letter \
//      -o fit-to-page=false tract-demo.pdf
// At true 100% the fold thirds stay exact. A consumer duplexer may land the
// back image up to ~1mm off the front; the package does not compensate, and the
// drift is harmless -- the two faces are different languages a reader never sees
// overlaid, and the cut still falls inside each face's margin.

// ---- the tract -------------------------------------------------------------
// One \tract call: front-language body, then back-language body. Each body is
// plain flowing prose; the package handles splitting it across the three panels
// and guards every split/copy with \isvoid so a body shorter than three columns
// simply leaves trailing panels blank instead of erroring, and a body longer
// than three columns emits a \message warning rather than crashing.

\tract{
\bold{God Loves You}

God created you and he loves you with an everlasting love. He made you for a
reason, he knows your name, and he has never once stopped caring about you. The
breath in your lungs and the longing in your heart for something more both point
back to him.

Yet the world is broken, and so are we. Each of us has turned away and gone our
own way, choosing self over the God who made us. The Bible calls this sin, and on
our own we can never bridge the gap it opens between us and God.

But God did not leave us there. He sent his only Son, Jesus, who lived a perfect
life, died on a cross in our place, and rose again on the third day, conquering
death itself. He took what we deserved so we could receive what only he deserves.

In him there is full forgiveness, new life, and lasting peace with God. Whoever
believes in him will not perish but have everlasting life. This gift cannot be
earned; it can only be received, with empty hands, by faith.

Will you turn to him today? Admit that you have sinned, believe that Jesus died
and rose for you, and call on him to save you. Everyone who calls on his name
will be saved, and he keeps every promise.
}{
\bold{Dios Te Ama}

Dios te creó y te ama con amor eterno. Te hizo con un propósito, conoce tu
nombre, y ni una sola vez ha dejado de cuidar de ti. El aliento en tus pulmones y
el anhelo en tu corazón de algo más apuntan de regreso a él.

Pero el mundo está quebrantado, y nosotros también. Cada uno se ha apartado y ha
seguido su propio camino, eligiéndose a sí mismo en lugar del Dios que lo hizo.
La Biblia llama a esto pecado, y por nosotros mismos jamás podremos cruzar el
abismo que abre entre nosotros y Dios.

Pero Dios no nos dejó así. Envió a su único Hijo, Jesús, quien vivió una vida
perfecta, murió en una cruz en nuestro lugar, y resucitó al tercer día, venciendo
a la muerte misma. Tomó lo que merecíamos para que recibiéramos lo que solo él
merece.

En él hay perdón completo, vida nueva, y paz duradera con Dios. Todo aquel que
cree en él no perecerá, sino que tendrá vida eterna. Este regalo no se gana; solo
se recibe, con las manos vacías, por la fe.

¿Te volverás a él hoy? Reconoce que has pecado, cree que Jesús murió y resucitó
por ti, e invócalo para que te salve. Todo el que invoque su nombre será salvo, y
él cumple cada promesa.
}
